  "text": "proprium.dominica-xii-post-pentecosten-evangelium",
  "about": "The evangelium of the formulary “12th Sunday after Pentecost” in the 1962 Roman Missal.",
  "segments": {
    "s01": { "translation": "At that time, Jesus said to His disciples: “Blessed are the eyes that see what you see. For I say to you that many prophets and kings desired to see what you see, and did not see it, and to hear what you hear, and did not hear it.” And behold, a certain expert in the law stood up, testing Him and saying: “Teacher, what shall I do to possess eternal life?” But He said to him: “What is written in the law? How do you read?” He answered and said: “You shall love the Lord your God with your whole heart, and with your whole soul, and with all your strength, and with all your mind, and your neighbor as yourself.” And He said to him: “You have answered rightly; do this, and you will live.” But he, wishing to justify himself, said to Jesus: “And who is my neighbor?” Then Jesus took this up and said: “A certain man was on his way down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and fell among robbers, who also stripped him, and having laid blows upon him, went away, leaving him half alive. Now it happened that a certain priest was going down by the same road, and when he saw him, he passed by. Likewise a Levite also, when he was near the place and saw him, passed on. But a certain Samaritan, as he journeyed, came near him, and seeing him, he was moved to mercy. And going up to him, he bound up his wounds, pouring in oil and wine; and setting him on his own beast, he brought him to an inn and took care of him. And the next day he took out two denarii and gave them to the innkeeper, and said: ‘Take care of him; and whatever you spend over and above, I myself will repay you when I return.’ Which of these three seems to you to have been neighbor to the man who fell among the robbers?” And he said: “The one who showed mercy to him.” And Jesus said to him: “Go, and you do likewise.”" }
  },
